  "unit": "pronouns",
  "title": "Suffix Pronouns",
  "short": "Suffix pronouns attach to the end of a word: =j \"I, my\", =k \"you, your\" (man), =ṯ (woman), =f \"he, his\", =s \"she, her\", =n, =ṯn, =sn.",
  "trouble": "Students write =f for \"she\". =f is \"he, his\"; =s is \"she, her\".",
  "prereqs": [
    "me1.gender"
  ],
  "xp": 2,
  "viz": [
    "eg-glyphs"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "practice": null,
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Egyptian has three sets of personal pronouns. The most frequent are the <b>suffix pronouns</b>, little endings attached to the end of a word. You already know two of their signs from the alphabet: the horned viper <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.6em;line-height:1\">𓆑</span> and the basket <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.6em;line-height:1\">𓎡</span>. Gender matters here, as in <a href=\"/egyptology/middle-egyptian-1/gender/\">Masculine and Feminine</a>."
      },
      {
        "t": "key",
        "html": "The suffix pronouns: <i class=\"eg-tl\">=j</i> I, my; <i class=\"eg-tl\">=k</i> you, your (to a man); <i class=\"eg-tl\">=ṯ</i> you, your (to a woman); <i class=\"eg-tl\">=f</i> he, his; <i class=\"eg-tl\">=s</i> she, her; <i class=\"eg-tl\">=n</i> we, our; <i class=\"eg-tl\">=ṯn</i> you, your (plural); <i class=\"eg-tl\">=sn</i> they, their."
      },
      {
        "t": "table",
        "head": [
          "Person",
          "Suffix",
          "Written",
          "Example",
          "Meaning"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "I",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">=j</i>",
            "<span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.5em;line-height:1\">𓀀</span> (or not written)",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">pr=j</i>",
            "my house"
          ],
          [
            "you (man)",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">=k</i>",
            "<span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.5em;line-height:1\">𓎡</span>",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">pr=k</i>",
            "your house"
          ],
          [
            "you (woman)",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">=ṯ</i>",
            "<span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.5em;line-height:1\">𓍿</span>",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">pr=ṯ</i>",
            "your house"
          ],
          [
            "he",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">=f</i>",
            "<span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.5em;line-height:1\">𓆑</span>",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">pr=f</i>",
            "his house"
          ],
          [
            "she",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">=s</i>",
            "<span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.5em;line-height:1\">𓋴</span>",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">pr=s</i>",
            "her house"
          ],
          [
            "we",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">=n</i>",
            "<span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.5em;line-height:1\">𓈖𓏥</span>",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">pr=n</i>",
            "our house"
          ],
          [
            "you (plural)",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">=ṯn</i>",
            "<span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.5em;line-height:1\">𓍿𓈖𓏥</span>",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">pr=ṯn</i>",
            "your house"
          ],
          [
            "they",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">=sn</i>",
            "<span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.5em;line-height:1\">𓋴𓈖𓏥</span>",
            "<i class=\"eg-tl\">pr=sn</i>",
            "their house"
          ]
        ],
        "cap": "The suffix pronouns (Gardiner, Lesson III). The plural forms add three strokes in writing."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "eg-glyphs",
        "opts": {
          "sp": "O1:Z1-I9",
          "parts": [
            [
              "O1",
              "logo",
              "pr"
            ],
            [
              "Z1",
              "stroke",
              ""
            ],
            [
              "I9",
              "phon",
              "f"
            ]
          ],
          "tl": "pr=f",
          "en": "his house",
          "breakdown": true
        },
        "cap": "pr=f “his house”: the noun, then the viper =f."
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "The first person is special. Its suffix <i class=\"eg-tl\">=j</i> is usually written with a picture of the speaker: a seated man <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.2em;line-height:1\">𓀀</span> if a man speaks, a seated woman <span class=\"eg-h\" style=\"font-size:1.2em;line-height:1\">𓁐</span> if a woman does, and a seated god if a god does. Often it is not written at all, and the reader supplies it from context. The = sign in transliteration simply shows where the suffix joins the word."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "Gender in the second and third persons"
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Egyptian distinguishes “you” said to a man (<i class=\"eg-tl\">=k</i>) from “you” said to a woman (<i class=\"eg-tl\">=ṯ</i>), and of course “he” (<i class=\"eg-tl\">=f</i>) from “she” (<i class=\"eg-tl\">=s</i>). In the plural there is no gender difference: <i class=\"eg-tl\">=ṯn</i> for any group addressed, <i class=\"eg-tl\">=sn</i> for any group spoken of."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "eg-glyphs",
        "opts": {
          "sp": "D21:N35-S29",
          "parts": [
            [
              "D21",
              "phon",
              "r"
            ],
            [
              "N35",
              "phon",
              "n"
            ],
            [
              "S29",
              "phon",
              "s"
            ]
          ],
          "tl": "rn=s",
          "en": "her name"
        },
        "cap": "rn=s “her name”."
      },
      {
        "t": "why",
        "html": "Why attach pronouns to words? Short, unstressed pronouns tend to lean on the word before them in many languages; in Egyptian they fused into suffixes. That is why they are tiny (one or two consonants) and why they can never stand alone or at the start of a sentence."
      },
      {
        "t": "mistake",
        "html": "Writing <i class=\"eg-tl\">=f</i> for “she”. <i class=\"eg-tl\">=f</i> (the viper) is “he, his”; <i class=\"eg-tl\">=s</i> (the folded cloth) is “she, her”. Check: f for the fellow, s for she."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "Possession",
        "q": "Write “his name”.",
        "steps": [
          "rn “name”.",
          "Suffix for “his”: =f."
        ],
        "a": "<i class=\"eg-tl\">rn=f</i>"
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "Read it",
        "q": "What does <i class=\"eg-tl\">jt=ṯ</i> mean?",
        "steps": [
          "jt “father”.",
          "=ṯ: “your”, said to a woman."
        ],
        "a": "“your father” (said to a woman)"
      },
      {
        "t": "try"
      }
    ],
